Chapter: HpetInterrupt
  cites Foreword chapter Board
  grounds Device.Mmio

 A comparator match reaches the processor.

 This is the rung the battery could not reach. codex-vm injects a tick
 every 55 ms from a host thread whatever the guest programmed, so a guest
 that programs a timer and then observes a tick has learned nothing --
 the tick was coming anyway. The only way to ask the question used to be
 to take the host's generator out of the picture with CODEX_VM_NO_TIMER,
 which is not a knob the harness can set, so this ran by hand or not at
 all.

 What made that necessary was not the host tick but the guest: nothing it
 could read recorded that any vector had arrived except the two timers.
 __interrupt_common answers the PIT and the local timer specially and
 returns from every other vector without leaving a mark, so a delivered
 interrupt and a dropped one were indistinguishable from inside.

 The handler now records the count and the number of every vector that is
 NOT a timer, and the timer vectors deliberately do not touch those cells.
 That is what lets this run in the ordinary battery: the host's periodic
 tick is vector 32 and cannot appear here, so a count above zero on the
 line this test programmed is evidence of that line and nothing else.

 The probe routes timer 0 to IOAPIC line 2 and gives that line vector 80,
 a number no other device in this machine uses. Serial interrupts are
 never injected and there is no keyboard timeline attached, so vector 80
 is the only one that can reach the counter -- and the test demands the
 recorded vector be exactly 80 rather than merely that something arrived.

 We say:

Section: Register Addresses

  hpet-config : Integer = #FED00010
  hpet-counter-lo : Integer = #FED000F0
  hpet-t0-config : Integer = #FED00100
  hpet-t0-cmp-lo : Integer = #FED00108

  ioapic-index : Integer = #FEC00000
  ioapic-data : Integer = #FEC00010

  devint-count-cell : Integer = 36240
  devint-last-vec-cell : Integer = 36248

Section: Routing

 Bit 2 of the timer configuration enables its interrupt and bits 9
 through 13 carry the line. Redirection entry 2 is the pair of selectors
 0x14 and 0x15; the low half takes the vector with delivery mode fixed
 and the mask clear.

  hpet-t0-route-2-enabled : Integer = #404
  ioapic-redir2-lo : Integer = #14
  ioapic-redir2-hi : Integer = #15
  probe-vector : Integer = 80

  route-timer : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  route-timer (x) = act
    s0 <- mmio-write-32 ioapic-index ioapic-redir2-lo
    d0 <- mmio-write-32 ioapic-data probe-vector
    s1 <- mmio-write-32 ioapic-index ioapic-redir2-hi
    d1 <- mmio-write-32 ioapic-data 0
    e <- mmio-write-32 hpet-config 1
    c <- mmio-write-32 hpet-t0-config hpet-t0-route-2-enabled
    x
  end

Section: Spin

 Each turn is a VM exit, which is what lets the host advance its device
 models and deliver anything it has queued.

  spin : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  spin (n) =
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else let v = mmio-read-32 hpet-counter-lo
    in v + spin (n - 1)

Section: Probe

  probe : Text
  probe = act
    r <- route-timer 0
    now <- mmio-read-32 hpet-counter-lo
    t <- mmio-write-32 hpet-t0-cmp-lo (now + 1000)
    before <- peek-qword devint-count-cell 0
    s <- spin 20000
    after <- peek-qword devint-count-cell 0
    vec <- peek-qword devint-last-vec-cell 0
    verdict before after vec
  end

  verdict : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  verdict (before) (after) (vec) =
    if after <= before then "hpet interrupt: never delivered"
    else if vec /= probe-vector then "hpet interrupt: wrong vector " & show vec
    else "hpet interrupt: delivered on vector 80"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Integer
  opening = act
    print-line-uni probe
    let r = 0 in r
  end
